  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Matter is anything that occupies space and has mass; weight depends on gravity and is not part of the definition.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Solids have fixed shape and volume because the particles are closely packed in a regular lattice and only vibrate.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sublimation is solid to gas directly, as with iodine, ammonium chloride and solid carbon dioxide.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Rusting produces a new substance, hydrated iron(III) oxide; the other three are physical changes.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Grinding changes only the size of the particles; no new substance is formed.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    An element contains only one kind of atom and cannot be broken down chemically.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A compound has a fixed composition and is formed by chemical combination; a mixture has a variable composition.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Air is a mixture of nitrogen, oxygen, argon, carbon dioxide and water vapour in variable proportions.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The solid is trapped on the filter paper and the liquid passes through as the filtrate.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Filtrate passes through; the residue stays on the paper.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The residue is the insoluble solid left on the filter paper.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Evaporation removes the solvent; crystallisation, by slow evaporation and cooling, gives purer crystals.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Crystallisation leaves impurities behind in the mother liquor, so it gives purer crystals than evaporating to dryness.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Simple distillation recovers the solvent from a solution of a non-volatile solute, for example water from salt solution.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The fractionating column allows repeated condensation and evaporation, so liquids of different boiling points are collected separately.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The two liquids form separate layers and are run off one at a time through the stopcock.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The more soluble a substance is in the solvent, the further it travels up the paper.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The solvent front is where the moving solvent stops; Rf is the distance moved by the spot divided by the distance to the solvent front.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Rf = distance moved by the spot ÷ distance moved by the solvent front; it is always less than 1.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Only iodine sublimes: on gentle heating it becomes a vapour which is then condensed as solid iodine.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Iron is magnetic and sulphur is not, so a magnet lifts the iron out of the mixture.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A pure substance has a sharp, constant melting point; an impurity spreads it over a range.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Impurities lower the melting point and make it less sharp — the standard test for purity.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Dissolved impurities raise the boiling point and lower the freezing point of a liquid.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Distillation leaves the dissolved salts behind and condenses pure water; evaporation to dryness would leave the salt, not the water.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Evaporating the water leaves the dissolved salt behind.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A dissolved solid passes through the filter paper with the liquid, so filtration cannot separate a solution.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The dyes have different solubilities, so they travel different distances up the chromatography paper and separate into bands.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Oil and water are immiscible and form two layers, which are run off separately through the stopcock of a separating funnel.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Impurities make the melting point lower and spread it over a range instead of leaving it sharp.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The two boiling points differ widely, so fractional distillation collects them separately.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Graphite contains only carbon atoms; steel, limestone and air are mixtures.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Slow evaporation followed by cooling gives crystals while impurities stay in solution.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ammonium chloride passes straight from solid to vapour on heating.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Dissolved impurities raise the boiling point.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Centrifugation separates by density using rapid spinning.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Rf = distance moved by dye ÷ 8 = 0.5, so the dye moved 4 cm.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Burning produces new substances — it is chemical.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Liquid particles are close but mobile, which is why liquids flow.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ammonium chloride sublimes away, leaving sodium chloride.
  ]

]

